    "Nxc3"
   ],
   "spoken": "Here's the payoff: with the knight on e2, the bishop capture on c3 is completely harmless — we just recapture with the knight. With a knight on f3 instead, that same capture actually wins a pawn, a very common blunder. And a bigger lesson: don't defend a pawn by pinning. Meeting a threat on e4 with a pin looks clever, but Black plays h6, and after the bishop retreats, g5 breaks the pin and wins the pawn outright — you can't recapture with the knight, so you've made two bishop moves only to swap it for a knight that moved once. The right, and nearly only, way to hold e4 is the queen to e2.",
   "kind": "plan",
   "teaches": [
    "don’t defend a pawn with a pin",
    "Qe2 holds e4"
   ]
  },
  {
   "ply": 8,
   "t": 628.5,
